1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Imagine you're sitting at home.
The door is locked.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:04,980
The windows are shut.

3
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:08,220
And suddenly, someone is standing
in your living room.

4
00:00:08,561 --> 00:00:11,501
They didn't pick the lock.
They didn't break through the wall.

5
00:00:12,141 --> 00:00:15,101
From where they stand,
nothing strange happened at all.

6
00:00:15,701 --> 00:00:17,641
They just took a route you can't take.

7
00:00:17,961 --> 00:00:20,301
Because suppose space had
one more direction.

8
00:00:20,381 --> 00:00:23,661
Not time. Not another universe.

9
00:00:23,941 --> 00:00:27,061
A real direction,
like left, forward, or up.

10
00:00:27,798 --> 00:00:29,318
It sounds like fantasy.

11
00:00:29,818 --> 00:00:34,678
But the trick behind it was worked
out in 1884, by a London headmaster.

12
00:00:35,448 --> 00:00:38,888
His name was Edwin Abbott,
and his book was called Flatland.

13
00:00:39,508 --> 00:00:41,268
Its narrator is a square.

14
00:00:42,048 --> 00:00:45,548
He lives on a flat plane,
and he has no idea that "up" exists.

15
00:00:46,364 --> 00:00:48,924
In Flatland, a house is a closed outline.

16
00:00:49,324 --> 00:00:51,864
A line drawn around you is a wall.

17
00:00:52,784 --> 00:00:54,764
Inside it, you are sealed off
from everyone.

18
00:00:55,203 --> 00:00:59,183
Then, one evening, a stranger appears
inside his closed house.

19
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:02,263
His wife can't understand how it got in.

20
00:01:02,583 --> 00:01:04,583
It's a sphere, from our world.

21
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:08,480
The square can only see the slice
that cuts through his plane.

22
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,120
So the sphere looks like a circle
that shrinks to a dot,

23
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:14,700
vanishes, and grows back out of nothing.

24
00:01:15,083 --> 00:01:17,963
And the sphere sees things
no one in Flatland can.

25
00:01:18,643 --> 00:01:21,183
Every house. Every locked chest.

26
00:01:21,583 --> 00:01:23,643
Even the square's own insides.

27
00:01:24,003 --> 00:01:25,943
All of it lying open to view.

28
00:01:26,492 --> 00:01:28,992
To prove it,
the sphere touches him in the stomach.

29
00:01:29,472 --> 00:01:33,072
From the inside.
Without crossing a single line.

30
00:01:33,495 --> 00:01:35,735
Then it lifts the square out of the plane.

31
00:01:36,315 --> 00:01:38,155
He doesn't pass through any wall.

32
00:01:38,715 --> 00:01:41,595
He goes up,
in a direction his world doesn't have.

33
00:01:42,235 --> 00:01:45,715
Now turn the book around.
We are the flat ones.

34
00:01:46,435 --> 00:01:50,175
A fourth direction would be one
we can't point to, or even picture.

35
00:01:51,095 --> 00:01:55,095
And to anyone who could move in it,
our walls would be like lines on paper.

36
00:01:56,284 --> 00:01:59,764
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

37
00:02:00,384 --> 00:02:02,004
It really helps the channel.

38
00:02:03,574 --> 00:02:06,414
So what could such a being do?
Think of a safe.

39
00:02:06,644 --> 00:02:08,314
Steel on every side.

40
00:02:08,663 --> 00:02:11,123
To a four-dimensional being,
it would be open,

41
00:02:11,343 --> 00:02:14,743
the way a square drawn on paper
is open to you, from above.

42
00:02:15,483 --> 00:02:19,603
It could see the money inside,
take it out, and leave the safe locked.

43
00:02:20,681 --> 00:02:24,261
Liu Cixin takes this idea
about as far as it goes.

44
00:02:24,841 --> 00:02:27,401
In Death's End,
the last book of the trilogy

45
00:02:27,631 --> 00:02:29,901
that begins with The Three-Body Problem,

46
00:02:30,081 --> 00:02:34,101
a spaceship crew drifts into a region
where space has four dimensions.

47
00:02:35,029 --> 00:02:39,349
Suddenly, they can see inside objects
that were completely sealed off before.

48
00:02:39,853 --> 00:02:44,113
It's fiction, of course.
But the geometry underneath is real.

49
00:02:45,394 --> 00:02:49,174
Now picture a four-dimensional object
passing through our world.

50
00:02:49,674 --> 00:02:51,374
We could never see all of it.

51
00:02:51,794 --> 00:02:54,114
Only the slice that's in our space,

52
00:02:54,194 --> 00:02:56,974
the way the square only saw
a slice of the sphere.

53
00:02:57,433 --> 00:03:00,493
A four-dimensional ball
would appear in your room as a dot.

54
00:03:00,833 --> 00:03:05,233
It would swell into a ball.
Then shrink. And vanish.

55
00:03:06,004 --> 00:03:10,364
A four-dimensional cube, pushed through
corner first, would be stranger still.

56
00:03:11,244 --> 00:03:14,904
A tiny pyramid that grows,
cuts off its own corners,

57
00:03:14,984 --> 00:03:18,844
and turns into an eight-sided diamond,
before shrinking away again.

58
00:03:19,171 --> 00:03:23,391
To us, it would look like a shape-shifting
object appearing out of thin air.

59
00:03:23,731 --> 00:03:26,791
To the object,
it's just moving in a straight line.

60
00:03:29,111 --> 00:03:31,691
And a fourth dimension
allows one more trick.

61
00:03:32,211 --> 00:03:36,551
Take your left shoe. However you turn it,
it never becomes a right shoe.

62
00:03:37,147 --> 00:03:40,127
They're mirror images,
and in three dimensions,

63
00:03:40,227 --> 00:03:42,867
no rotation gets you
from one to the other.

64
00:03:43,365 --> 00:03:45,925
But think of a paper cutout
of the letter R.

65
00:03:46,745 --> 00:03:49,985
Slide it around the table all you like,
it stays an R.

66
00:03:50,665 --> 00:03:53,505
Lift it off the table,
flip it over, and it's backwards.

67
00:03:54,188 --> 00:04:00,148
In 1827, the mathematician August Möbius
noticed the same thing one level up.

68
00:04:00,588 --> 00:04:06,088
In a space with four dimensions, an object
could be turned into its own mirror image.

69
00:04:06,809 --> 00:04:08,849
Lift your left shoe
into the fourth direction,

70
00:04:08,929 --> 00:04:12,129
turn it, set it back down,
and it's a right shoe.

71
00:04:12,865 --> 00:04:15,445
H. G. Wells turned that into a story.

72
00:04:16,265 --> 00:04:17,845
In The Plattner Story, from 1896,

73
00:04:18,075 --> 00:04:22,025
a schoolteacher is blown out of our space
by an explosion.

74
00:04:22,519 --> 00:04:25,939
When he comes back, his heart beats
on the right side of his body.

75
00:04:26,559 --> 00:04:29,899
And he can only write
from right to left, with his left hand.

76
00:04:32,559 --> 00:04:35,779
So far, a fourth dimension
sounds like a superpower.

77
00:04:36,079 --> 00:04:37,599
But there's a catch.

78
00:04:38,553 --> 00:04:41,753
A fourth direction wouldn't just be
somewhere for us to go.

79
00:04:42,253 --> 00:04:44,913
Everything else could spread into it too.

80
00:04:45,233 --> 00:04:46,573
Including gravity.

81
00:04:47,463 --> 00:04:49,963
In our world, gravity follows
a simple rule.

82
00:04:50,043 --> 00:04:53,463
Twice as far away,
and it's four times weaker.

83
00:04:54,196 --> 00:04:55,396
Here's why.

84
00:04:55,816 --> 00:04:58,776
Picture the Sun's pull
spreading out in every direction,

85
00:04:58,856 --> 00:05:00,876
over the surface of a growing sphere.

86
00:05:01,169 --> 00:05:04,569
Double the distance,
and that surface is four times larger.

87
00:05:05,309 --> 00:05:07,689
The same pull is spread
four times thinner.

88
00:05:08,307 --> 00:05:10,387
Now give space a fourth direction.

89
00:05:11,267 --> 00:05:14,507
The pull spreads over the surface
of a four-dimensional sphere.

90
00:05:14,727 --> 00:05:17,027
And that surface grows faster.

91
00:05:17,667 --> 00:05:19,247
With the cube of the distance.

92
00:05:19,814 --> 00:05:21,374
Twice as far, eight times weaker.

93
00:05:21,604 --> 00:05:25,294
Ten times as far, a thousand times weaker.

94
00:05:26,035 --> 00:05:28,655
That sounds like a detail. It isn't.

95
00:05:29,824 --> 00:05:35,044
In 1917, the physicist Paul Ehrenfest
asked what this would do to the planets.

96
00:05:35,944 --> 00:05:39,964
Working with Newton's laws,
he found there would be no stable orbits.

97
00:05:40,781 --> 00:05:43,801
A planet stays in orbit
because two things balance.

98
00:05:44,581 --> 00:05:46,161
Gravity pulls it in.

99
00:05:46,581 --> 00:05:48,701
Its own motion carries it outward.

100
00:05:49,257 --> 00:05:51,737
In our space,
if a planet drifts a little farther out,

101
00:05:51,967 --> 00:05:55,057
that outward push fades faster
than gravity does.

102
00:05:55,897 --> 00:05:57,637
So the planet is pulled back.

103
00:05:58,277 --> 00:05:59,937
The orbit corrects itself.

104
00:06:00,557 --> 00:06:03,117
With a fourth dimension,
in that simple picture,

105
00:06:03,197 --> 00:06:05,597
both fade at exactly the same rate.

106
00:06:06,297 --> 00:06:08,077
Nothing corrects anything.

107
00:06:08,833 --> 00:06:12,493
A little too slow,
and the planet spirals into its star.

108
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:16,332
A little too fast,
and it drifts away for ever.

109
00:06:17,433 --> 00:06:19,593
Ehrenfest argued
that atoms would have the same problem,

110
00:06:19,823 --> 00:06:22,513
because the electric force
that holds them together

111
00:06:22,743 --> 00:06:25,373
would spread out the same way.

112
00:06:25,533 --> 00:06:28,053
Later physicists, using quantum mechanics,

113
00:06:28,133 --> 00:06:32,453
came to the same conclusion,
at least with electricity as we know it.

114
00:06:32,995 --> 00:06:36,415
So a big fourth dimension
wouldn't just give us a new direction.

115
00:06:37,055 --> 00:06:39,475
It could take away the solar system.

116
00:06:39,635 --> 00:06:42,195
And maybe the atoms we're made of.

117
00:06:42,537 --> 00:06:45,537
Which means this is no longer
a story about magic.

118
00:06:46,257 --> 00:06:47,457
It's a clue.

119
00:06:49,717 --> 00:06:53,357
Earth has been circling the Sun
for four and a half billion years.

120
00:06:53,977 --> 00:06:55,577
It hasn't spiralled in.

121
00:06:55,837 --> 00:06:57,397
It hasn't drifted off.

122
00:06:57,709 --> 00:07:01,409
So a large fourth dimension,
one that ordinary matter can move through

123
00:07:01,639 --> 00:07:03,709
and gravity can freely spread into,

124
00:07:03,789 --> 00:07:07,229
is in sharp conflict
with the universe we actually see.

125
00:07:09,759 --> 00:07:13,439
But that's not the same as saying
there's no fourth dimension at all.

126
00:07:14,219 --> 00:07:17,099
Physicists have found
at least two places it could hide.

127
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,950
The first idea is a century old.

128
00:07:20,130 --> 00:07:24,410
In 1921, Theodor Kaluza
added a fourth direction of space

129
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:26,150
to Einstein's equations.

130
00:07:26,735 --> 00:07:31,355
And in 1926, Oskar Klein suggested
why we don't notice it.

131
00:07:31,995 --> 00:07:35,555
It's curled up into a circle,
far too small to see.

132
00:07:35,934 --> 00:07:38,834
Think of a garden hose
seen from across a field.

133
00:07:39,354 --> 00:07:40,754
It looks like a line.

134
00:07:41,194 --> 00:07:43,614
Only up close do you see that it's round.

135
00:07:45,454 --> 00:07:47,314
The second idea is newer.

136
00:07:48,054 --> 00:07:50,114
What if the extra direction is large,

137
00:07:50,194 --> 00:07:53,494
but we're stuck to our three dimensions,
like ink on a page?

138
00:07:54,077 --> 00:07:57,037
That answers a question
Flatland never asks.

139
00:07:57,717 --> 00:08:01,797
A direction can exist, and you still
might not be able to step into it.

140
00:08:02,103 --> 00:08:04,903
Atoms, light, people,
all of it could be stuck.

141
00:08:05,503 --> 00:08:07,723
And maybe only gravity leaks out.

142
00:08:08,745 --> 00:08:13,645
In 1998, three physicists pointed out
that extra dimensions like that

143
00:08:13,875 --> 00:08:17,505
could be as large as a millimetre,
and nobody would have noticed.

144
00:08:18,065 --> 00:08:21,825
Because nobody had measured gravity
at such small distances.

145
00:08:22,446 --> 00:08:24,546
So people went and measured it.

146
00:08:25,106 --> 00:08:26,466
Today, gravity's simple rule

147
00:08:26,696 --> 00:08:29,586
has been checked at distances
thinner than a human hair.

148
00:08:30,106 --> 00:08:31,966
So far, it still holds.

149
00:08:32,701 --> 00:08:35,681
At the Large Hadron Collider,
physicists look for energy

150
00:08:35,911 --> 00:08:37,821
that goes missing from a collision,

151
00:08:38,001 --> 00:08:40,661
as if something had slipped
out of our three dimensions.

152
00:08:41,691 --> 00:08:46,021
So far, no convincing evidence
of extra dimensions has emerged.

153
00:08:48,631 --> 00:08:50,391
So here's where we are.

154
00:08:51,091 --> 00:08:53,231
A big fourth dimension,
the kind you could walk into,

155
00:08:53,461 --> 00:08:55,311
doesn't fit the universe we see.

156
00:08:55,931 --> 00:08:59,071
A small one, or a hidden one,
is still an open question.

157
00:08:59,711 --> 00:09:01,151
And it's one we can test.

158
00:09:03,821 --> 00:09:06,981
Remember the square.
He spent his whole life

159
00:09:07,211 --> 00:09:09,901
in a world with a missing direction,
and never noticed,

160
00:09:10,131 --> 00:09:12,161
until something came to visit.

161
00:09:12,993 --> 00:09:16,773
If our world has a missing direction,
nothing has come to visit.

162
00:09:17,433 --> 00:09:19,033
Not that we've noticed.

163
00:09:19,813 --> 00:09:23,553
So the real question isn't only
what the world would look like from there.

164
00:09:24,301 --> 00:09:26,881
It's why, if it's there,
we still haven't found it.

165
00:09:26,961 --> 00:09:28,951
That's where we're going next.
